# Copy to .env in this directory (the project root, next to run_airon.py):
#
#     cp .env.example .env
#
# .env is gitignored. Nothing in it is ever printed - startup lists the
# names it loaded, never the values.

# The conversational brain. Without this aiRon still sees, recognises,
# greets people and asks names; it just cannot hold a conversation.
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-...

# --- The conversational brain's settings ----------------------------------
#
# All four are optional; unset means the value shown. They exist so a
# different configuration can be tried without editing code on the robot,
# and what they change is only audible from in front of it - so change one
# at a time and listen. Startup prints whichever set is in force.

# Which model does the talking. claude-opus-5 is the one aiRon's personality
# was written against; claude-sonnet-5 and claude-opus-4-8 are worth a
# listen. Note that aiRon sends an effort setting (below), and models that
# predate it - claude-haiku-4-5 among them - reject the request outright
# rather than ignoring it. A wrong model id is not caught at startup either:
# aiRon comes up as normal, then goes quiet every time somebody speaks, with
# the reason in the log as "[brain] model call failed".
# AIRON_LLM_MODEL=claude-opus-5

# How hard it may think before answering: low, medium, high, xhigh, max.
# This is the pause-against-quality dial, and the pause is the thing that
# decides whether aiRon feels conversational or feels like a voice
# assistant. Measured once on short German prompts, the whole range came
# back well inside the timeout below - low in 4.9 s, max in 5.7 s - so the
# dial is cheaper than it looks, but that was one link and one short
# exchange rather than a real conversation.
# AIRON_LLM_EFFORT=low

# Ceiling on one spoken reply, in tokens. A ceiling, not a target - raising
# it does not make aiRon talkative, but it stops a long answer being cut off
# mid-sentence, which out loud sounds like the robot being interrupted.
# AIRON_LLM_MAX_TOKENS=400

# How long to wait for a reply before giving up, in seconds. Past this the
# moment has gone and somebody is standing there waiting, so it is short on
# purpose. Worth raising if replies start being abandoned - an answer lost
# on the way back sounds exactly like a model that had nothing to say, so
# the log is the only place the difference shows.
# AIRON_LLM_TIMEOUT=12

# Where the robot is, so it can answer about tomorrow's weather. Either a
# place name, resolved once, or coordinates - coordinates win if both are
# set. Unset means aiRon says it cannot reach the weather rather than
# guessing at a location.
#
# The forecast comes from Open-Meteo, which needs no key and no account:
# nothing about this feature requires a credential to exist, so nothing
# about it can leak one.
# AIRON_WEATHER_PLACE=Düsseldorf
# AIRON_WEATHER_LATLON=51.23,6.78

# Letting aiRon look things up on the web, through Tavily (AIRON-26). Unset
# means aiRon says it cannot look that up, rather than answering from what
# the model happens to remember - which is the whole point of searching.
#
# Unlike the weather above, this one does need a credential, and it is the
# only tool that sends text the model composed to a third party. What comes
# back is quoted to the model rather than obeyed, and the call that reads it
# has no tools in its schema at all - see airon/world/search.py.
# TAVILY_API_KEY=tvly-...
